      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Thermal joins and conditional contact hypotheses",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For two simple domains $\\Gamma_i\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_i+1}$ and $\\Gamma_j\\subset\\mathbb R^{n_j+1}$, write their coordinates $(u_i,v_i)$ and $(u_j,v_j)$. Their thermal-join domain is\n\n$$\\Delta_{ij}:=\\{(u,v_i,v_j):\\exists u_i,u_j,\\ (u_i,v_i)\\in\\Gamma_i,\\ (u_j,v_j)\\in\\Gamma_j,\\ u=u_i+u_j\\}.$$\n\nThe joined simple type is a distinct formal tag with amount function $\\nu_{\\Delta_{ij}}=\\nu_{\\Gamma_i}+\\nu_{\\Gamma_j}$ and weight $w_{\\Delta_{ij}}=w_{\\Gamma_i}+w_{\\Gamma_j}$, as defined in [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Encode the separated pair by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and a joined coordinate $Z$ by $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(Z)$. Their bookkeeping masses agree, and their aggregate composition functions agree. These encoded maps and their raw tags remain distinct. Positive coordinate-scaled copies use the reversible preparation transport of that definition; nested joins preserve their finite primitive amount function under reassociation and permutation. The empty type contributes the zero map.\n\nWrite $J_{ij}(x,y)$ for the join state with the summed energy coordinate and the unchanged work coordinates. A compound pair $(x,y)$ and $J_{ij}(x,y)$ have the same total energy and work coordinates but are distinct tagged objects. Define $x\\sim_T y$ exactly when $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_i}(x)+\\mathsf{J}_{\\Gamma_j}(y)$ and $\\mathsf{J}_{\\Delta_{ij}}(J_{ij}(x,y))$ are accessible in both directions. Nested joins use the declared reassociation and permutation identifications.\n\nAssume every finite join is itself a simple domain satisfying [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. The contact hypotheses are mathematical assumptions on the common relation: (T1) $(x,y)\\prec J_{ij}(x,y)$; (T2) every join state has at least one reversible split into component states at its stated coordinates, and the equal-state split of any state into positive scaled copies of itself is reversible; (T3) $\\sim_T$ is transitive; (T4) for every simple state $x$ there are states $x_0,x_1$ in its domain with $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$ and $x_0\\sim_T x_1$; (T5) for every ordered pair of simple systems, every $x$ in the first and every work coordinate $v$ in the second's projection $\\rho(\\Gamma_j):=\\{v:\\exists u,(u,v)\\in\\Gamma_j\\}$, there is a state $y$ with work coordinate $v$ and $x\\sim_T y$.\n\nFor the completed calibrator branch, add (C1) **join coherence**: whenever $x\\sim_T y$, the join state $J_{ij}(x,y)$ is thermally equivalent to each of $x$ and $y$ under the nested-join identifications. The coordinate sum in the definition alone does not assert C1. In later theorem statements, T1–T5 and C1 are explicitly listed as hypotheses; they are not treated as established physical laws.",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.A, pp. 51–54",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Relative interiors of finite-dimensional convex sets",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft local prerequisite carrier authored; scoped checks pass; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $C$ be a nonempty convex subset of a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$. Its relative interior $\\operatorname{ri}_H C$ is the interior in $H=\\operatorname{aff}C$. Then $\\operatorname{ri}_H C$ is nonempty. If $w\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$, $y\\in\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$, and $0\\le t<1$, then $(1-t)w+ty\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Let $k=\\dim(\\operatorname{aff}C)$. If $k=0$, then $C$ is a singleton and is open in its affine hull. If $k>0$, the definition of affine hull supplies $k+1$ affinely independent points of $C$ spanning it. Their simplex lies in $C$ by [F1]. Its barycenter has all $k+1$ barycentric coordinates positive; sufficiently small coordinate perturbations preserve positivity and their sum one. Thus a relative neighborhood of that barycenter lies in the simplex, proving $\\operatorname{ri}_H C\\ne\\varnothing$. [given, F1, construct]\n\n2.1 Fix $w,y,t$ as in the Statement. The case $t=0$ is $w\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$. Suppose $0<t<1$ and put $q=(1-t)w+ty$. Choose $r>0$ so the relative ball $B_H(w,r)$ lies in $C$. Since $y\\in\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$, choose $y_m\\in C$ converging to $y$. For any $q'$ in a sufficiently small relative ball about $q$, the point $w_m(q'):=(q'-ty_m)/(1-t)$ lies in $B_H(w,r)$ once that ball is chosen and then $m$ is large. Hence $q'=(1-t)w_m(q')+ty_m\\in C$ by [F1]. A relative neighborhood of $q$ lies in $C$, so $q\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H C$. [given, F1, choose, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§III.B–III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Supporting hyperplanes at relative boundary points",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft local prerequisite carrier authored; scoped checks pass; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $C$ be a nonempty convex set in a finite-dimensional affine Euclidean space $H$ of dimension $k\\ge1$, let $\\operatorname{aff}C=H$, and put $K=\\overline{C}^{\\,H}$. If $z\\in\\partial_H K$ (that is, $z\\in K$ and every relative neighborhood of $z$ meets $H\\setminus K$), then there is a nonzero linear functional $\\ell$ on the translation space of $H$ such that $\\ell(q-z)\\le0$ for all $q\\in C$. If $C\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ has affine dimension at most $d-2$, then every $z\\in C$ has at least two distinct ambient supporting hyperplanes containing $\\operatorname{aff}C$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Choose affine coordinates identifying $H$ with $\\mathbb R^k$. Convexity of $C$ makes its closure $K$ convex: approximate any two points of $K$ by sequences in $C$ and pass to the limit in each convex combination. Since $z\\in\\partial_H K$, choose $z_m\\notin K$ with $z_m\\to z$. By [F1], for each $m$ there are $u_m\\ne0$ and $c_m$ with $u_m\\cdot q\\le c_m<u_m\\cdot z_m$ for all $q\\in K$. Normalize $u_m$ to unit length; [F3] places these vectors on the compact unit sphere, which is compact by [F2], so a subsequence converges to a unit vector $u$. [F2] and [F3] provide the convergent subsequence. [given, F1, F2, F3, choose]\n\n2.1 For every $q\\in K$, the inequalities give $u_m\\cdot(q-z_m)<0$. Along the convergent subsequence, continuity of the Euclidean inner product yields $u\\cdot(q-z)\\le0$. The limit vector has unit length, so its functional is nonzero and supports $K$, hence $C$, at $z$. [step 1.1, F3, algebra]\n\n3.1 If $C\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ has affine hull $H$ of dimension at most $d-2$, its orthogonal complement has dimension at least two. Choose two nonparallel nonzero normals $n_1,n_2$ orthogonal to the direction space of $H$. For each $j$, the hyperplane $\\{q:n_j\\cdot(q-z)=0\\}$ contains $H$ and therefore supports $C$ at $z$. The two hyperplanes are distinct because their normals are not parallel. [given, F3, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-unique-support-inward-ray",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Interior rays from a unique supporting plane",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft local prerequisite carrier authored; scoped checks pass; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-closed-convex-separation": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be closed and convex with nonempty interior, $d\\ge1$, and let $x\\in\\partial K$. Suppose $K$ has exactly one supporting hyperplane at $x$. Choose its inward unit normal $n$, so $n\\cdot(q-x)\\ge0$ for every $q\\in K$. Then for every $v$ with $n\\cdot v>0$, there is $\\delta>0$ such that $x+tv\\in\\operatorname{int}K$ for every $0<t<\\delta$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Let $T=\\overline{\\{a(q-x):a\\ge0,\\ q\\in K\\}}$. This is a closed convex cone. If a nonzero functional $\\ell$ is nonnegative on $T$, then it is nonnegative on $K-x$, so its zero plane through $x$ supports $K$. Uniqueness in the hypothesis makes that plane the plane with normal $n$; the inward sign forces $\\ell=c n\\cdot(\\,\\cdot\\,)$ for some $c>0$. [given, F2]\n\n2.1 The support at $x$ gives $T\\subseteq\\{w:n\\cdot w\\ge0\\}$. If $v$ with $n\\cdot v>0$ were outside $T$, strict separation [F1] of $v$ from the cone would give a nonzero functional $\\ell$ nonnegative on $T$ and negative at $v$: the separating bound is zero because $0\\in T$ and $T$ is a cone. Step 1.1 then makes $\\ell$ a positive multiple of $n$, contradicting $n\\cdot v>0$. Thus the open half-space $n\\cdot w>0$ lies in $T$, and each such $v$ is an interior point of $T$. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Fix such $v$. Since $v\\in\\operatorname{int}T$, choose $d+1$ affinely independent vectors in a small ball about $v$ whose simplex contains a neighborhood of $v$. Approximate each vector by a nonzero cone generator $g_j=a_j(q_j-x)$ with $a_j>0$, $q_j\\in K$. Sufficiently close approximations preserve affine independence and keep $v$ in the simplex interior, so a neighborhood $N$ of $v$ lies in the simplex of the $g_j$. For each $w\\in N$, write $w=\\sum_j\\theta_j(w)a_j(q_j-x)$ with $\\theta_j(w)\\ge0$ and $\\sum_j\\theta_j(w)=1$. Shrink $N$ so its closure is a closed bounded neighborhood of $v$ contained in that simplex. Barycentric coordinates vary continuously there; by [F3] the closure is compact, so $A:=\\sup_{w\\in N}\\sum_j\\theta_j(w)a_j<\\infty$. [step 2.1, construct, F2, F3]\n\n4.1 Choose $\\delta>0$ with $\\delta A<1$. For $0<t<\\delta$ and each $w\\in N$, $x+tw=(1-t\\sum_j\\theta_j a_j)x+\\sum_j t\\theta_j a_j q_j$ is a convex combination of points of $K$, hence lies in $K$ by [F4]. Therefore $x+tN\\subset K$, so a neighborhood of $x+tv$ lies in $K$ and $x+tv\\in\\operatorname{int}K$. [step 3.1, given, F4, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-convex-function-local-lipschitz",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Finite convex functions are locally Lipschitz",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft local prerequisite carrier authored; scoped checks pass; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $U\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be open and convex, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be finite and convex. For every $x_0\\in U$, there are a neighborhood $V$ of $x_0$ and $L<\\infty$ such that $|f(x)-f(y)|\\le L\\|x-y\\|$ for all $x,y\\in V$. The same conclusion holds for finite concave functions by applying the result to $-f$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Choose a closed cube centered at $x_0$ whose concentric dilation by factor $2$ lies in $U$. Every point of the cube is a convex combination of its finitely many vertices, so [F1] and convexity bound $f$ above there by the largest vertex value $M$. If $x$ lies in the cube, then $2x_0-x$ lies in the dilated cube; convexity gives $f(x_0)\\le(f(x)+f(2x_0-x))/2$, hence $f(x)\\ge2f(x_0)-M'$, where $M'$ is the finite upper bound on the dilated cube. Thus $f$ is bounded above and below on a ball about $x_0$. [given, F1, construct]\n\n2.1 Choose concentric balls $B(x_0,r)\\subset B(x_0,2r)\\subset U$ on which $m\\le f\\le M$. For distinct $x,y\\in B(x_0,r/2)$, extend the segment from $x$ through $y$ to $z=y+(r/2)(y-x)/\\|y-x\\|$, which lies in $B(x_0,2r)$. Put $\\theta=\\|y-x\\|/(r/2+\\|y-x\\|)$, so $y=(1-\\theta)x+\\theta z$. Convexity yields $f(y)-f(x)\\le\\theta(M-m)\\le(2(M-m)/r)\\|y-x\\|$. Repeating with $x,y$ interchanged gives the reverse bound. Negation proves the concave case. [step 1.1, F1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-integral-zero-uniqueness",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Zero-start integral inequality",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft local prerequisite carrier authored; scoped checks pass; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "deps": [
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "thm-chain-rule",
          "cor-mean-value-theorem",
          "thm-derivative-of-exponential"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-algebra-of-derivatives": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-mean-value-theorem": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-derivative-of-exponential": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $L\\ge0$, $T>0$, and let $f:[0,T]\\to[0,\\infty)$ be continuous. If $f(t)\\le L\\int_0^t f(s)\\,ds$ for every $t\\in[0,T]$, then $f(t)=0$ for every $t\\in[0,T]$.",
        "proof": "1.1 By [F1], the integral defining $h(t)=\\int_0^t f(s)\\,ds$ exists. Continuity of $f$ gives $h'(t)=f(t)$ on $(0,T)$: the difference quotient error is bounded by $\\sup_{|s-t|\\le|r|}|f(s)-f(t)|$, which tends to zero as $r\\to0$. Also $h$ is continuous on $[0,T]$. The hypothesis gives $h'\\le Lh$, so [F2]–[F3] yield $g'(t)\\le0$ for $g(t)=e^{-Lt}h(t)$ on $(0,T)$. The mean value theorem makes $g$ nonincreasing; since $g(0)=0$ and $g\\ge0$, $g\\equiv0$ and $h\\equiv0$. [given, F1, F2, F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 For any $t>0$, the nonnegative continuous function $f$ has integral $h(t)=0$. If $f(t_0)>0$ at any $t_0>0$, continuity gives a positive lower bound on a nonempty subinterval of $[0,t_0]$, contradicting $h(t_0)=0$. At $t=0$ the assumed inequality gives $f(0)\\le0$; nonnegativity gives $f(0)=0$. Hence $f\\equiv0$. [step 1.1, given] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Appendix 9.9, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Spectator cancellation without comparison completeness",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-tdmath-compound-mass-well-defined": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Under A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], for compounds $A,B$ with $m(A)=m(B)$ and every finite spectator compound $C$, $A+C\\prec B+C$ if and only if $A\\prec B$. Consequently, for finite-support signed maps $u,v$ of equal total coefficient, define $u\\preceq_s v$ by $(v-u)^-\\prec(v-u)^+$. This relation is well-defined, agrees with $\\prec$ on nonnegative maps, is invariant under adding a common signed map, is transitive, and is closed under addition of two comparisons. No comparison hypothesis is used.",
        "proof": "1.1 Assume $A+C\\prec B+C$ and $m(C)>0$. Scaling by $1/n$ gives $A/n+C/n\\prec B/n+C/n$. Define $E_j=(n-j)A/n+jB/n+C/n$ for $j=0,\\ldots,n$. For $j<n$, add the common spectator $(n-j-1)A/n+jB/n$ to the scaled comparison to obtain $E_j\\prec E_{j+1}$. Transitivity yields $A+C/n=E_0\\prec E_n=B+C/n$. Stability with bases $A,B$, fixed positive spectators $C,C$, and $\\epsilon_n=1/n$ gives $A\\prec B$. If $m(C)=0$, nonnegativity gives $C=0$ and the implication is immediate. Conversely, consistency gives $A+C\\prec B+C$ from $A\\prec B$. [A1, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 For equal-total signed maps $u,v$, the difference $d=v-u$ has finite support and total coefficient zero, so $m(d^+)=m(d^-)$. If $u,v$ are nonnegative, their pointwise common part $k(x)=\\min\\{u(x),v(x)\\}$ gives $u=k+d^-$ and $v=k+d^+$. Cancellation in step 1.1 proves $u\\prec v$ exactly when $d^-\\prec d^+$, which by [F2] is $u\\preceq_s v$; adding a common signed map leaves $d$ unchanged by [F2]. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 If $u\\preceq_s v$ and $v\\preceq_s w$, [F2] identifies these with comparisons of $(v-u)^-$ to $(v-u)^+$ and $(w-v)^-$ to $(w-v)^+$. Consistency combines them. Their pointwise common minimum cancels by step 1.1, leaving $(w-u)^-\\prec(w-u)^+$, which [F2] identifies as $u\\preceq_s w$. The same finite-sum argument proves closure under addition; reflexivity follows from reflexivity of the zero compound. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 2.1 and §III.C",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Signed comparison for forward-sector relations",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $u,v$ be finite-support real coefficient maps on the tagged state labels of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]] with equal total coefficient. For a typed state $x$, compound notation uses the represented map $\\mathsf{J}(x)$ from [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]; its raw unit point mass is $e_{(\\Gamma,x)}$. Signed maps still use the ordinary total of their raw coefficients, exactly the bookkeeping mass of the represented nonnegative maps. Put $d=v-u$, $d^+(x)=\\max(d(x),0)$ and $d^-(x)=\\max(-d(x),0)$. Define $u\\preceq_s v$ to mean $d^-\\prec d^+$ under the compound relation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Write $u\\sim_s v$ when both $u\\preceq_s v$ and $v\\preceq_s u$. These are formal signed comparison maps, not physical negative amounts. The positive and negative parts are finite-support compounds of equal mass. If $u\\sim_s v$, then $cu\\sim_s cv$ for every real $c$: positive $c$ uses scaling, $c=0$ is reflexivity, and negative $c$ swaps the positive and negative parts while equivalence supplies both comparison directions. This Definition introduces the signed notation; ordinary-order compatibility and the signed-order laws are proved separately.",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §II.D",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §4.1, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-thermal-scaling-invariance",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Scaling invariance of thermal equivalence",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Under T1–T3 and the equal-state reversible split in [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]], if $x\\sim_T y$ then $s x\\sim_T t y$ for every $s,t>0$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Let $x$ be a unit state and $s>0$. The state $z=(1+s)x$ in the scaled type has the equal-state reversible split with fractions $1/(1+s)$ and $s/(1+s)$; its two components are exactly $x$ and $sx$. Thus $(x,sx)$ is reversible with its join, which by definition is $x\\sim_T sx$. The same argument gives $y\\sim_T ty$. [A1, given]\n\n2.1 Since $x\\sim_T y$, symmetry gives $y\\sim_T x$. Transitivity with $x\\sim_T sx$ yields $y\\sim_T sx$; symmetry then gives $sx\\sim_T y$. Transitivity with $y\\sim_T ty$ now yields $sx\\sim_T ty$. [A1, step 1.1, given] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 4.1, p. 51",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-reference-strip-coordinate",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "A unique reference coordinate inside a thermal strip",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 9,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
          "lem-tdmath-thermal-scaling-invariance",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-thermal-scaling-invariance": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let a simple domain and its finite thermal joins satisfy [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]]. Let $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ with $x_0\\sim_T x_1$, and let $x$ satisfy $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$. For $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, write $R_\\lambda$ for the compound state $((1-\\lambda)x_0,\\lambda x_1)$, omitting a zero coefficient at the endpoints. Then there is a unique $\\lambda\\in(0,1)$ with $R_\\lambda\\sim x$ in the common accessibility relation.",
        "proof": "**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F3], and $x_0\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec x_1$.\n\n1.1 For $0\\le\\lambda<\\mu\\le1$, the signed difference $R_\\mu-R_\\lambda=(\\mu-\\lambda)(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})$ has positive part $(\\mu-\\lambda)e_{x_1}$ and negative part $(\\mu-\\lambda)e_{x_0}$. Since $x_0\\prec x_1$, positive scaling and [F2] give $R_\\lambda\\prec R_\\mu$. Thus the reference states are strictly ordered by parameter. [A1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 For $0<\\lambda<1$, [F1] makes $(1-\\lambda)x_0$ and $\\lambda x_1$ thermally equivalent. By the definition of thermal equivalence, their compound is reversible with its join state. The equal-state split makes $((1-\\lambda)x,\\lambda x)$ reversible with its join state and with $x$. Both join states belong to the join of two scaled copies of the same simple domain, so [F3] compares them. The endpoint cases use $R_0=x_0$ and $R_1=x_1$ directly. Hence $R_\\lambda$ and $x$ are comparable for every $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$. [F1, F3, given]\n\n2.1 Let $L=\\{\\lambda\\in[0,1]:R_\\lambda\\prec x\\}$. The strict endpoint comparison $x_0\\prec x$ puts $0$ in $L$. Step 1.1 and transitivity make $L$ downward closed. Let $s=\\sup L$. If $s>0$, then $s-1/n\\in L$ for every sufficiently large $n$. Since $R_{s-1/n}+(1/n)x_1=R_s+(1/n)x_0$, consistency yields $R_s+(1/n)x_0\\prec x+(1/n)x_1$. Stability with bases $R_s,x$ and fixed spectators $x_0,x_1$ gives $R_s\\prec x$. If $s=0$, this comparison is the given $x_0\\prec x$. [A1, F2, step 1.1, choose]\n\n3.1 If $s<1$, then $s+1/n\\notin L$ for every sufficiently large $n$. Step 1.2 gives comparability and the definition of $L$ excludes $R_{s+1/n}\\prec x$, so $x\\prec R_{s+1/n}$. The identity $R_{s+1/n}+(1/n)x_0=R_s+(1/n)x_1$ and stability yield $x\\prec R_s$. If $s=1$, the strict strip gives $x\\prec R_1$; together with step 2.1 this would imply $x_1\\prec x$, contrary to $x\\prec\\prec x_1$. Thus $s<1$, and steps 2.1 and 3.1 give $R_s\\sim x$. [A1, F2, step 1.2, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 The equivalence $R_s\\sim x$ also excludes $s=0$, because $R_0=x_0$ and $x_0\\not\\sim x$, and excludes $s=1$ by strictness of $x\\prec\\prec x_1$. If $R_t\\sim x$ for another $t$, transitivity gives $R_s\\sim R_t$; step 1.1 then forces $s=t$. Hence the parameter is unique and lies in $(0,1)$. [F2, step 1.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §IV.B.1, pp. 54–56",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Convexity and relative closedness of forward sectors",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], for every simple state $x$, the forward sector $A_x=\\{y\\in\\Gamma_i:x\\prec y\\}$ is convex and closed relative to $\\Gamma_i$. For collinear $y=tx+(1-t)z$, $0<t<1$, $y\\prec z$ implies $x\\prec y$, and $x\\prec z$ implies $x\\prec y$.",
        "proof": "1.1 If $x\\prec y$ and $x\\prec z$, splitting gives $x\\sim(tx,(1-t)x)$. Consistency, positive scaling, and transitivity then give $(tx,(1-t)x)\\prec(ty,(1-t)z)$. The convex-combination access hypothesis in [A1] gives $(ty,(1-t)z)\\prec ty+(1-t)z$, so the convex combination belongs to $A_x$. [A1, algebra]\n\n1.2 Let $y=tx+(1-t)z$ with $0<t<1$. The convex-combination access hypothesis in [A1] gives $(tx,(1-t)z)\\prec y$. Splitting gives $y\\sim(ty,(1-t)y)$. If $y\\prec z$, scaling by $1-t$ and adding $ty$ gives $(ty,(1-t)y)\\prec(ty,(1-t)z)$. Compose and cancel the common $(1-t)z$ by [F2], then scale by $1/t$, to get $x\\prec y$. If instead $x\\prec z$, scaling by $1-t$ and adding $tx$ gives $(tx,(1-t)x)\\prec(tx,(1-t)z)\\prec y$; recombination gives $x\\prec y$. [A1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 Take $y\\in\\overline{A_x}^{\\,\\Gamma_i}$. If $y\\in A_x$, the claim is immediate. Otherwise choose $w\\in\\operatorname{ri} A_x$ by [F1]. Because $\\Gamma_i$ is open, choose $z=w+(1+\\eta)(y-w)\\in\\Gamma_i$ for some $\\eta>0$. For every integer $n>1+\\eta$, $y_n=(1+1/n)y-z/n=w+(1-\\eta/n)(y-w)$ lies in $\\operatorname{ri}A_x$ by [F1], so $x\\prec y_n$. Also $y=\\frac{n}{n+1}y_n+\\frac1{n+1}z$. The convex-combination access hypothesis in [A1] gives $(\\frac n{n+1}y_n,\\frac1{n+1}z)\\prec y$; scaling by $(n+1)/n$ and using the reversible split of $y$ gives $(y_n,z/n)\\prec(y,y/n)$. [A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Consistency adds the same spectator $z/n$ to $x\\prec y_n$, and transitivity with step 2.1 gives $(x,z/n)\\prec(y,y/n)$ for every $n>1+\\eta$. Apply the stability hypothesis in [A1] to bases $x,y$, fixed unit spectators $z,y$, and the tail sequence $\\epsilon_m=1/(m+n_0)$ with $n_0>1+\\eta$. It follows that $x\\prec y$. Thus every relative closure point belongs to $A_x$. [A1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Lemma 3.1 and Theorem 3.2, pp. 42–45",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-sector-interior",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Every forward sector has nonempty interior",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], every forward sector $A_x$ has nonempty interior in its ambient simple domain $\\Gamma_i$.",
        "proof": "1.1 Suppose $A_x$ has empty ambient interior. If $d=1$, S1 supplies a strict successor $y\\ne x$; convexity puts the nondegenerate interval between $x$ and $y$ in $A_x$, contradicting empty interior. Thus take $d\\ge2$. The affine hull of $A_x$ has dimension at most $d-1$. If its dimension were at most $d-2$, [F2] would give two distinct supporting hyperplanes at $x$, contradicting S2. Hence its affine hull is a hyperplane $H$; this $H$ supports $A_x$ at every point, including $x$. [A1, F2]\n\n1.2 By S1 choose $y$ with $x\\prec\\prec y$. Then $y\\in A_x$ and $x\\notin A_y$. Transitivity gives $A_y\\subseteq A_x\\subseteq H$. If $A_y$ had affine dimension at most $d-2$, [F2] would again give multiple ambient supports at $y$, contrary to S2. Thus $\\operatorname{aff}A_y=H$. [A1, F1, F2]\n\n1.3 Choose $w\\in\\operatorname{ri}_H A_y$ by [F3]. The segment from $w$ to $x$ is contained in $H\\cap\\Gamma_i$ by convexity of both sets, starts in $A_y$, and ends outside $A_y$. Its intersection with $A_y$ is closed by relative closedness [F1], so it has a last point $z$ before $x$. Points immediately after $z$ on this segment lie outside $A_y$ and, because $A_y$ is closed in the open set $\\Gamma_i$, outside $\\overline{A_y}^{\\,H}$. Thus $z\\in\\partial_H\\overline{A_y}^{\\,H}$. The support statement [F2] supplies a nonzero functional on $H$ whose zero hyperplane supports $A_y$ at $z$. [F1, F2, F3, choose]\n\n2.1 Extend this functional to the ambient Euclidean space by taking its normal vector in the direction space of $H$. Its zero hyperplane contains the within-$H$ support and is not $H$ itself. Since $z\\in A_y$, transitivity gives $A_z\\subseteq A_y$, so the extended plane supports $A_z$ at $z$; the plane $H$ also supports $A_z$. These are distinct, contradicting S2 at $z$. Therefore $A_x$ has nonempty ambient interior. [A1, F1, step 1.3, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 3.2, pp. 43–45",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Nesting and comparison of simple-system states",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness",
          "lem-tdmath-sector-interior",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-sector-interior": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-interior-segment": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], the sectors are nested: if $y\\in A_x$, then $A_y\\subseteq A_x$; equality holds when $y\\in\\partial A_x$, and $A_y\\subset\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$ when $y\\in\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$. For every $x,y\\in\\Gamma_i$, either $x\\prec y$ or $y\\prec x$ (possibly both). Strict comparison is exactly interior membership: $x\\prec\\prec y$ if and only if $y\\in\\operatorname{int}_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$.",
        "proof": "1.1 If $y\\in A_x$, transitivity gives $A_y\\subseteq A_x$. If also $y\\in\\partial A_x$, [F3] gives equality. Suppose instead $y\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$. If $A_y$ met $\\partial A_x$ at $z$, then $z\\in A_y$ and [F3] would give $A_z=A_x$. Since $z\\in A_y$, transitivity gives $A_z\\subseteq A_y\\subseteq A_x$, so $A_y=A_x$. But S2 puts $y$ on $\\partial A_y$, contradicting $y\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$. Thus $A_y$ avoids $\\partial A_x$ and, being a subset of the closed sector $A_x$, lies in $\\operatorname{int}A_x$. [A1, F3, algebra]\n\n1.2 Suppose neither $y\\in A_x$ nor $x\\in A_y$. Choose $z\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$ using [F2]. The segment from $y$ to $z$ lies in $\\Gamma_i$ and meets $\\partial A_x$ at a point $w$ strictly between its endpoints. By [F3], $A_w=A_x$, so $w\\prec z$. The collinear implication in [F1], applied to $w=ty+(1-t)z$, gives $y\\prec w\\prec z$. Hence every $z\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$ lies in $A_y$. Reversing $x,y$ gives $\\operatorname{int}A_y\\subseteq A_x$. [A1, F1, F2, F3]\n\n2.1 Because $A_x$ is full-dimensional by [F2], its affine hull is the ambient space. Fix $w\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$. For any $q\\in A_x$, relative closedness gives $q\\in\\overline{A_x}^{\\,\\Gamma_i}$, and [F4] puts $(1-t)w+tq$ in $\\operatorname{int}A_x$ for every $0\\le t<1$. Letting $t\\uparrow1$ shows $q\\in\\overline{\\operatorname{int}A_x}^{\\,\\Gamma_i}$. The reverse inclusion follows because $A_x$ is relatively closed. Hence $A_x=\\overline{\\operatorname{int}A_x}^{\\,\\Gamma_i}$; the same holds for $A_y$. Relative closedness from [F1] therefore extends the two interior inclusions in step 1.2 to $A_x\\subseteq A_y$ and $A_y\\subseteq A_x$. Equality would put $x\\in A_y$, contradicting the supposition. Thus at least one of $x\\prec y$ or $y\\prec x$ always holds. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.2]\n\n3.1 If $x\\prec y$, then $y\\in A_x$. If $y$ is a boundary point, step 1.1 gives $A_x=A_y$, hence $x\\prec y$ and $y\\prec x$. Otherwise step 1.1 gives $A_y\\subset\\operatorname{int}A_x$, and $x\\notin A_y$ because S2 places $x$ on $\\partial A_x$. Thus $x\\prec\\prec y$. Conversely, if $y\\in\\operatorname{int}A_x$, step 1.1 and S2 show $x\\notin A_y$, so $x\\prec\\prec y$. [A1, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorems 3.6–3.8, pp. 47–50",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Forward orientation and the Lipschitz boundary graph",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness",
          "lem-tdmath-sector-interior",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support",
          "lem-tdmath-unique-support-inward-ray",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-function-local-lipschitz",
          "lem-tdmath-integral-zero-uniqueness",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "thm-chain-rule",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative",
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-sector-closedness": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-sector-interior": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-relative-boundary-support": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-unique-support-inward-ray": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-convex-function-local-lipschitz": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-integral-zero-uniqueness": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-chain-rule": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-newton-leibniz-with-interior-derivative": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-continuous-implies-integrable": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], each simple domain has one orientation sign $\\varepsilon_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, constant across the domain, such that at fixed work coordinate $v$, $x\\prec y$ exactly when $\\varepsilon_i(u_y-u_x)\\ge0$. In coordinates oriented so $\\varepsilon_i=1$, for each $x\\in\\Gamma_i$ the projection $\\rho_x$ of $\\partial_{\\Gamma_i}A_x$ onto work coordinates is open and connected; the boundary is the graph $(u_x(v),v)$ of a $C^1$ function with locally Lipschitz derivative\n\n$$Du_x(v)=-P_i(u_x(v),v).$$\n\nIf $y\\in\\partial A_x$, then $A_y=A_x$ and their boundary graphs agree.",
        "proof": "**Given:** [A1] and [F1]–[F10]. Write a support plane's inward unit normal with energy component nonzero, as ensured by its finite-slope form.\n\n1.1 For fixed $x=(u_x,v_x)$, the vertical section $I_x:=\\{u:(u,v_x)\\in A_x\\}$ is a relatively closed convex interval by [F1]. The support plane at $x$ confines it to one side of $u_x$. Its inward normal has nonzero energy component, so [F4] gives a nontrivial interior vertical segment on that side. [A1, F1, F4]\n\n1.2 The fiber orientation is locally constant in $v$. At any $x$, choose one vertical point just inside the supported half-space and one just outside; by [F4] the inside point can be chosen in $\\operatorname{int}A_x$. Openness of $\\Gamma_i$ and of the interior and exterior regions preserves these memberships on nearby fibers. Each such fiber therefore meets $\\partial A_x$ between these two energy values. A support of $A_x$ at that boundary point also supports the point's own sector and so has its S2 orientation; the inside/outside placement fixes that orientation. Thus nearby fibers have the same sign as the fiber through $x$. Since $\\Gamma_i$ is convex, it is connected; a locally constant two-valued sign is constant. [A1, F2, F3, F4]\n\n1.3 Let $u,w$ be two $C^1$ graph solutions of $Dq=-P_i(q,v)$ on a common open domain, and suppose $u(v_0)=w(v_0)$. Since $P_i$ is locally Lipschitz, choose a convex neighborhood of $(u(v_0),v_0)$ on which one Lipschitz constant $L$ works. Continuity of $u,w$ lets us choose a convex ball about $v_0$ whose closure lies in the common domain and whose two graph images stay in that neighborhood; take any $v$ in a smaller concentric ball. Put $d=v-v_0$ and $\\gamma(t)=v_0+td$ for $0\\le t\\le1$. By [F8], $r(t)=u(\\gamma(t))-w(\\gamma(t))$ is $C^1$ and\n$r'(t)=[-P_i(u(\\gamma(t)),\\gamma(t))+P_i(w(\\gamma(t)),\\gamma(t))]\\cdot d.$\nThe local Lipschitz bound for $P_i$ gives $|r'(t)|\\le L\\|d\\||r(t)|$. The derivative is continuous, hence integrable by [F10]; [F9] gives $r(t)=\\int_0^t r'(s)\\,ds$; hence $h(t):=|r(t)|\\le L\\|d\\|\\int_0^t h(s)\\,ds$. Apply [F6] on $[0,1]$ to obtain $h=0$. Thus $u=w$ near $v_0$. Along any polygonal path in the common open domain, the set of path parameters at which equality holds is closed by continuity and open by this local result; since the path interval is connected and the set is nonempty, equality holds along the whole path. Every open connected subset of Euclidean space is polygonally connected: points reachable from a fixed point by finitely many segments in small balls form a nonempty relatively open and relatively closed set. Therefore equality extends throughout each common connected domain. [A1, F6, F8, F9, F10, given, construct]\n\n2.1 The convex, relatively closed interval $I_x$ contains a nontrivial segment on the inward side by step 1.1 and [F4]. Suppose it does not contain the whole open $\\Gamma_i$-fiber on that side. Its endpoint $b$ toward that side then lies inside $\\Gamma_i$; relative closedness puts $b$ in $I_x$, so the interval from $x$ to $b$ is a compact nondegenerate interval by [F7]. Let $x_1$ be its midpoint. Since $x_1\\in A_x$, transitivity gives $A_{x_1}\\subseteq A_x$. The supporting plane of $A_{x_1}$ has nonzero energy coefficient, so its vertical section lies wholly on one side of $x_1$; being contained in $I_x$, it lies in one half of $[x,b]$ and has at most half its length. It is nonempty and closed relative to $[x,b]$, hence compact. Repeat: take the midpoint $x_{k+1}$ of the current compact interval $I_{x_k}$; then $x_k\\prec x_{k+1}$, so the next section is nested inside the current one and again has at most half its length. The nested compact intervals have a common limit point $x_\\infty\\in\\Gamma_i$ by [F7], and their lengths tend to zero. Relative closedness puts $x_\\infty$ in every $A_{x_k}$, hence $A_{x_\\infty}\\subseteq A_{x_k}$ by transitivity. Its vertical section is therefore only $\\{x_\\infty\\}$, contradicting the nontrivial interior vertical segment at $x_\\infty$ supplied by [F4]. Thus $I_x$ contains the whole open fiber on its inward side. [A1, F1, F4, F7, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 All states on a fixed work-coordinate fiber have the same orientation. If $x$ is oriented upward and a state $z$ above it were oriented downward, the full upward section from step 2.1 gives $x\\prec z$, and the full downward section from $z$ gives $z\\prec w$ for a state $w$ below $x$, contradicting the support at $x$. If a state $y$ below an upward $x$ were oriented downward, choose $z$ above $x$ with $x\\prec z$; the first collinear implication in [F1], applied to $x$ between $y$ and $z$, gives $y\\prec x$, contradicting the downward support at $y$. The reversed argument handles a downward $x$. [F1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Adopt the coordinate convention $\\varepsilon_i=1$. Step 2.1 and the common orientation give $x\\prec y$ exactly for $u_y\\ge u_x$ at fixed $v$, with strict comparison for $u_y>u_x$. [step 3.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n5.1 Put $\\rho_x=\\pi_v(\\partial A_x)$. At a boundary point $z\\in\\partial A_x$, [F3] supplies a support to $A_x$. Because $A_z\\subseteq A_x$, that plane also supports $A_z$ and equals its unique S2 plane. Its finite slope gives a nonvertical inward direction; [F4] supplies nearby vertical points inside $A_x$, while the opposite side is outside by the support. Neighborhoods of one inside and one outside point show every nearby work fiber meets $\\partial A_x$. Hence $\\rho_x$ is open. It is connected because S3 makes $\\partial A_x$ arcwise connected and projection is continuous. By step 4.1, every vertical fiber over $\\rho_x$ has exactly one boundary energy; call it $u_x(v)$. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, step 4.1]\n\n6.1 For $v,v'\\in\\rho_x$, convexity of $A_x$ puts every convex combination of their boundary states in $A_x$. On any sufficiently small convex ball in $\\rho_x$, the unique-boundary property and upward orientation make $u_x$ the least energy in each fiber. Therefore $u_x(tv+(1-t)v')\\le t u_x(v)+(1-t)u_x(v')$, so $u_x$ is finite and convex there. It is locally Lipschitz by [F5]. The inward support at $(u_x(v),v)$ gives\n$u_x(v')-u_x(v)\\ge -P_i(u_x(v),v)\\cdot(v'-v).$\nThe support at $(u_x(v'),v')$ gives\n$u_x(v')-u_x(v)\\le -P_i(u_x(v'),v')\\cdot(v'-v).$\nLocal Lipschitz continuity of $u_x$ and $P_i$ makes the difference between these linear bounds $O(\\|v'-v\\|^2)$. Thus $u_x$ is differentiable with derivative $-P_i(u_x(v),v)$, and that derivative is locally Lipschitz. Hence $u_x\\in C^1$ with locally Lipschitz derivative. [A1, F1, F5, step 5.1]\n\n7.1 If $y\\in\\partial A_x$, then $A_y\\subseteq A_x$. The boundary graphs agree at $v_y$ and solve the same differential equation. The set of $v\\in\\rho_y$ where $v\\in\\rho_x$ and $u_y(v)=u_x(v)$ is nonempty, open by step 1.3, and closed by continuity and relative closedness of $\\partial A_x$. Connectedness of $\\rho_y$ makes it all of $\\rho_y$. This shows $\\rho_y\\subseteq\\rho_x$. The same argument, using relative closedness of $\\partial A_y$, shows $\\rho_y$ is relatively closed in connected $\\rho_x$; it is relatively open because both projections are open. Hence $\\rho_x=\\rho_y$ and the boundary graphs agree. In particular $x\\in\\partial A_y$, so $y\\prec x$; together with $y\\in A_x$ and transitivity this gives $A_x=A_y$. [A1, F1, step 6.1, step 1.3] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorems 3.3–3.5, pp. 45–47",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§4.1 and 7.2–7.3, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-reference-strip-gluing",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Reference-strip gluing proves comparison for scaled copies",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 10,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
          "lem-tdmath-reference-strip-coordinate",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-reference-strip-coordinate": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and T1–T4 of [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]] for every finite join. For any one simple type $\\Gamma_i$, every two finite compounds of its scaled copies with the same total coefficient mass are comparable. Thus CH holds on all multiple scaled products of that type. No entropy across different material types is asserted here.",
        "proof": "1.1 By T4, the open strips $\\Sigma(a,b):=\\{x:a\\prec\\prec x\\prec\\prec b\\}$ with $a\\prec\\prec b$ and $a\\sim_T b$ cover $\\Gamma_i$. They are open: by [F2], $a\\prec\\prec x$ is $x\\in\\operatorname{int}A_a$, and $x\\prec\\prec b$ is $x\\notin A_b$; the latter set is open because $A_b$ is relatively closed. [A1, A2, F2]\n\n2.1 Let $\\Sigma(a,b)$ and $\\Sigma(c,d)$ overlap. Their intersection is open. Choose two states $e\\prec\\prec f$ in a small vertical segment contained in the overlap, using [F4]. Let $s_e,s_f$ be their coordinates in the first strip and $t_e,t_f$ in the second, supplied by [F1]. If $s_f<s_e$, step 1.1 gives $R_{s_f}\\prec R_{s_e}$, so $f\\prec e$ by the two reference equivalences; if $s_f=s_e$, those references coincide and give $f\\sim e$. Both contradict $e\\prec\\prec f$. Hence $s_f>s_e$, and the same argument gives $t_f>t_e$. [A1, F1, F2, F4, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 The four reference equivalences give, in signed comparison,\n$f-e\\sim_s(s_f-s_e)(b-a),\\qquad f-e\\sim_s(t_f-t_e)(d-c).$\nPut $r=(s_f-s_e)/(t_f-t_e)>0$. Additivity and positive scaling in [F3] give $d-c\\sim_s r(b-a)$. Applying the same algebra to the equivalences for $e$ gives $c\\sim_s a+(s_e-r t_e)(b-a)$. Consequently a state in the second strip with coordinate $t$ has first-chart coordinate $s_e+r(t-t_e)$. The change of chart is affine with strictly positive slope. [F1, F3, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The same calculation covers a finite chain of overlapping strips: after each overlap, express the new strip's endpoint states as signed-equivalent affine maps in the existing reference pair. The local strip lemma gives every state $z$ of the added strip a coordinate $s_z\\in\\mathbb R$ with $e_z\\sim_s(1-s_z)e_a+s_ze_b$ in the existing chart. Signed addition, positive scaling, translation, and equivalence under negative scaling are valid by [F3]; hence induction along a finite overlap chain gives one such common chart for every state in the union. [F3, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 Take any finite list of target states in $\\Gamma_i$ and join them in order by finitely many straight segments, which lie in $\\Gamma_i$ by convexity. The resulting connected path is a finite union of closed bounded segments, hence compact by [F5]. The open strips from step 1.1 cover it, so compactness gives a finite subcover. The intersection graph of those strips meeting the path is connected: otherwise their unions would partition the connected path into two disjoint nonempty relatively open sets. Order a spanning tree from one root strip; each later strip overlaps an earlier one in an open set and can be glued by step 4.1. Thus every target state has a common signed chart $e_z\\sim_s(1-s_z)e_a+s_ze_b$ with $a\\prec\\prec b$. [A1, F5, step 1.1, step 4.1, construct]\n\n6.1 Let $A=\\sum_j\\alpha_j e_{x_j}$ and $B=\\sum_k\\beta_k e_{y_k}$ have equal total mass $M$, and let $s_j,t_k$ be their common-chart coordinates. If $M=0$, nonnegativity makes both maps empty and reflexivity compares them. Assume $M>0$. Each state has signed equivalence $e_{x_j}\\sim_s(1-s_j)e_a+s_je_b$ in the common chart; signed addition, scaling, and splitting [F3] therefore make $A$ and $B$ each signed-equivalent to reference maps $R_{q_A}=(M-q_A)e_a+q_Ae_b$ and $R_{q_B}=(M-q_B)e_a+q_Be_b$, where $q_A=\\sum_j\\alpha_js_j$ and $q_B=\\sum_k\\beta_kt_k$. If $q_A<q_B$, the signed difference has positive part $(q_B-q_A)e_b$ and negative part $(q_B-q_A)e_a$, so $a\\prec b$ and positive scaling compare $R_{q_A}\\prec R_{q_B}$ by [F3]. If $q_A=q_B$, the reference maps coincide and reflexivity applies; if $q_B<q_A$, the same argument compares in the reverse direction. Transitivity transfers that comparison to $A,B$. Hence any equal-mass pair is comparable, proving CH. [A1, F3, step 5.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 4.4 and Lemma 4.1, pp. 54–56",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-contact-energy-orientation",
        "reference": "items/lem-tdmath-contact-energy-orientation.md",
        "design_state": "Owner escalation prerequisite authored; draft with independent pipeline review pending.",
