            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapter 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Contact calibrators under explicit join coherence",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 12,
        "deps": [
          "lem-tdmath-contact-energy-orientation",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph",
          "thm-tdmath-reference-strip-gluing",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "lem-tdmath-thermal-class-dichotomy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-tdmath-contact-energy-orientation": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-sector-nesting": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-forward-boundary-graph": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-reference-strip-gluing": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-thermal-class-dichotomy": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma_2$ be simple domains in a family of finite thermal joins satisfying A1–A7, S1–S3, T1–T5, and C1 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and [[def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses]]. Then there are $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$ in $\\Gamma_1$ and $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$ in $\\Gamma_2$ such that\n\n$$(x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0).$$\n\nMoreover, for every fixed finite composition of scaled copies of $\\Gamma_1$ and $\\Gamma_2$, any two equal-mass states of that same composition are comparable (CH). The conclusion is conditional on the explicitly stated join-coherence and universal-range assumptions C1 and T5.",
        "proof": "**Given:** [A1]–[A2] and [F1]–[F5].\n\n1.1 Apply [F1] and [F2] to each simple type, including every finite join. The convex-combination access hypothesis in [A1] makes each resulting entropy concave, since the comparison of a scaled pair with its convex-combination state translates under the representation to the concavity inequality. By [F4], each entropy is strictly increasing with energy at fixed work coordinates. [A1, F1, F2, F4]\n\n1.2 Use T5 to choose $\\bar x\\in\\Gamma_1$ and $\\bar y\\in\\Gamma_2$ with $\\bar x\\sim_T\\bar y$. Let $\\phi=J_{12}(\\bar x,\\bar y)$ and consider its adiabat $\\partial A_\\phi$. T2 gives every $Z$ on that adiabat at least one split $Z=J_{12}(x_Z,y_Z)$ with $x_Z\\sim_T y_Z$. [A2, F4, choose]\n\n2.1 Suppose a join state $Z$ on $\\partial A_\\phi$ has two distinct equilibrium splits $(x^{(1)},y^{(1)})$ and $(x^{(2)},y^{(2)})$. By the definition of $\\sim_T$, both pairs are reversible with $Z$, hence mutually accessible compounds. Their work coordinates are fixed by $Z$ and their total energies equal its energy. If the first-component energies agreed, both component states in the splits would agree, so distinctness forces unequal first-component energies. If $u_{x^{(1)}}<u_{x^{(2)}}$, fixed-work comparison [F4] gives $x^{(1)}\\prec\\prec x^{(2)}$; conservation of total energy gives $u_{y^{(2)}}<u_{y^{(1)}}$, hence $y^{(2)}\\prec\\prec y^{(1)}$. Taking $(x_0,x_1,y_0,y_1)=(x^{(1)},x^{(2)},y^{(2)},y^{(1)})$ gives $(x_0,y_1)\\sim(x_1,y_0)$ because both split compounds are reversible with $Z$. If the first energies occur in the opposite order, swap the two splits and obtain the same calibrator form. Therefore, if no calibrator exists, every $Z\\in\\partial A_\\phi$ has a unique equilibrium split. [A2, F4, step 1.2, choose]\n\n3.1 Assume no calibrator exists. For any equilibrium split $(x_Z,y_Z)$ of $Z\\in\\partial A_\\phi$, reversibility with $Z$ and the common adiabat give $(x_Z,y_Z)\\sim(\\bar x,\\bar y)$ as compounds. By [F1], $x_Z$ and $\\bar x$ are comparable; [F4] says their sectors coincide exactly when they are equivalent. If $x_Z\\prec\\prec\\bar x$, pair reversibility gives $(\\bar x,\\bar y)\\prec(x_Z,y_Z)$. Consistency adds $\\bar y$ to $x_Z\\prec\\bar x$, and cancellation [F5] gives $\\bar y\\prec y_Z$. If $y_Z\\prec\\bar y$, consistency and the pair comparison instead cancel to give $\\bar x\\prec x_Z$, a contradiction. Thus $\\bar y\\prec\\prec y_Z$, making a calibrator. The case $\\bar x\\prec\\prec x_Z$ is symmetric: it gives $y_Z\\prec\\prec\\bar y$, again a calibrator. Since neither is allowed under the assumption, $x_Z\\sim\\bar x$. Adding spectators to the two directions and cancelling by [F5] then gives both $\\bar y\\prec y_Z$ and $y_Z\\prec\\bar y$, so $y_Z\\sim\\bar y$. Thus every split on this adiabat has components in the corresponding component adiabats. [A1, A2, F1, F4, F5, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 Under the no-calibrator assumption, fix a first-component work coordinate $v_1$ in the projection of $\\partial A_{\\bar x}$. The boundary-graph result [F4] gives exactly one state $x_*$ on that component adiabat at $v_1$. Every split of a join state on $\\partial A_\\phi$ with first work coordinate $v_1$ therefore has first component $x_*$. Each split is thermal, so $x_*\\sim_T y_Z$ for its second component. T3 makes all such $y_Z$ thermally equivalent, and C1 makes each joined state $J(x_*,y_Z)=Z$ thermally equivalent to $x_*$. Hence all join states on this adiabat over fixed $v_1$ lie in one thermal class. Fixing a second-component work coordinate instead gives the corresponding conclusion for first components. [A2, F4, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 Let $\\Omega$ be the open connected work-coordinate projection of $\\partial A_\\phi$ from [F4]. From any point of $\\Omega$, the set reachable by finitely many coordinate-axis segments contained in open boxes in $\\Omega$ is relatively open, and its complement is also relatively open; connectedness makes the reachable set all of $\\Omega$. Along each such segment, step 4.1 gives equivalence of the corresponding join states; T3 composes these finitely many equivalences. Thus the whole adiabat $\\partial A_\\phi$ is one thermal class. Since the argument works for every starting join state, every join adiabat is one thermal class. [A2, F4, step 4.1, construct]\n\n6.1 Still assuming no calibrator, apply [F3] to each join system. Its alternative with two thermally inequivalent states on one adiabat is excluded by step 5.1, so all states of each join system are thermally equivalent. For any $x,x'\\in\\Gamma_1$, use T5 with one allowed second work coordinate to choose $y,y'$ with $x\\sim_T y$ and $x'\\sim_T y'$. C1 relates each component to its joined state, and all join states are in one thermal class; T3 therefore gives $x\\sim_T x'$. The same argument gives one thermal class in $\\Gamma_2$. Now take $Z=\\phi\\in\\partial A_\\phi$ and a split $(x,y)$ supplied by T2. Choose nonzero sufficiently small $\\delta$ so $(u_x+\\delta,v_1)\\in\\Gamma_1$ and $(u_y-\\delta,v_2)\\in\\Gamma_2$, possible because the domains are open. These states form a distinct split of the same $Z$. Since the original split has $x\\sim_T y$ and each component type is one thermal class, T3 gives thermal equivalence of the changed pair as well. This contradicts uniqueness of the split from step 2.1. Therefore a calibrator exists. [A2, F3, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 5.1, choose]\n\n7.1 Fix a calibrator $x_0\\prec\\prec x_1$, $y_0\\prec\\prec y_1$, whose existence is step 6.1. Choose same-type entropy representatives $S_1,S_2$ from [F2], with positive gaps $h_1=S_1(x_1)-S_1(x_0)$ and $h_2=S_2(y_1)-S_2(y_0)$. Normalize $\\widetilde S_i=S_i/h_i$, so both calibrator increments are one, and put $s(x)=\\widetilde S_1(x)-\\widetilde S_1(x_0)$ and $t(y)=\\widetilde S_2(y)-\\widetilde S_2(y_0)$. Write $u=e_{x_0}+e_{y_1}$ and $v=e_{x_1}+e_{y_0}$. The calibrator says $u\\sim v$, so $v-u=(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})-(e_{y_1}-e_{y_0})$ is equivalent to zero; translation and cancellation [F5] give $(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})\\sim_s(e_{y_1}-e_{y_0})$. For any state $x$, the signed map $d_x=e_x-(1-s(x))e_{x_0}-s(x)e_{x_1}$ has total mass and $\\widetilde S_1$-value zero. Its positive and negative parts have equal mass and entropy, so [F2] makes them equivalent. Hence $e_x\\sim_s(1-s(x))e_{x_0}+s(x)e_{x_1}$; the same argument applies to states of the second type. These are formal signed maps when a coefficient lies outside $[0,1]$. [F2, F5, step 6.1, algebra]\n\n8.1 For a state pair $(x,y)$, put $\\lambda=(s(x)+t(y))/2$ and $\\delta=(s(x)-t(y))/2$. Adding the signed reductions from step 7.1 makes the pair equivalent to $(1-\\lambda)(e_{x_0}+e_{y_0})+\\lambda(e_{x_1}+e_{y_1})$ plus $\\delta[(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})-(e_{y_1}-e_{y_0})]$. The calibrator increment equivalence from step 7.1 makes the latter term equivalent to zero for either sign of $\\delta$. Thus each pair reduces to its reference map at parameter $\\lambda$. For any finite compound $A$ with amounts $M_1,M_2$ of the two types, add and scale these single-state reductions. Writing $q_1(A),q_2(A)$ for the amount-weighted sums of $s(x),t(y)$, respectively, gives $A\\sim_s(M_1-q_1(A))e_{x_0}+q_1(A)e_{x_1}+(M_2-q_2(A))e_{y_0}+q_2(A)e_{y_1}$. Using $(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})\\sim_s(e_{y_1}-e_{y_0})$, this is equivalent to $M_1e_{x_0}+M_2e_{y_0}+Q(A)(e_{x_1}-e_{x_0})$, where $Q(A)=q_1(A)+q_2(A)$. If the total mass is zero, both compounds are empty and reflexivity compares them. Otherwise, for same-composition $A,B$, their base terms agree. If $Q(A)<Q(B)$, the positive and negative parts of the reference-map difference are $(Q(B)-Q(A))e_{x_1}$ and $(Q(B)-Q(A))e_{x_0}$, so $x_0\\prec x_1$ and positive scaling compares the maps forward. If the Q values agree the maps coincide; if $Q(A)>Q(B)$ the reverse comparison holds. Translation, addition, and ordinary-order compatibility [F5] transfer the result to $A,B$. This proves CH for every fixed finite composition of the two types and their scaled copies. [A1, F2, F5, step 7.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorems 4.5–4.8, pp. 55–59",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Calibration of an arbitrary family of simple-system entropies",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-thermal-contact-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-signed-comparison": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $I$ be a nonempty set of primitive simple-system labels containing a distinguished meter label $m$. Assume every finite separated product is explicitly represented by its finitely many tagged elementary factors; positive scaling and finite product are the only identifications. Suppose the join-closed family satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-conditional-contact-calibrator]], and fix a strict meter pair $z_0\\prec\\prec z_1$ in $\\Gamma_m$. Assume the Axiom of Choice. Then one can choose, for every $i\\in I$, an additive constant and positive scale for its same-type entropy so that\n\n$$S\\big((t_1i_1,x_1),\\ldots,(t_ni_n,x_n)\\big)=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^n t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$$\n\nis additive, extensive, and characterizes every same-composition comparison on every finite separated product. No conclusion is asserted for transitions that change composition.",
        "proof": "**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F3].\n\n1.1 Apply [F2] to the meter type and choose its entropy $S_m$ so $S_m(z_0)=0$ and $S_m(z_1)=1$. Set the meter reference to $r_m=z_0$, and use [A1] to choose a reference state $r_i$ and one same-type entropy representative for every $i\\ne m$. For each $i\\ne m$, use [F1] to choose calibrator states $a_i,b_i\\in\\Gamma_i$ and $c_i,d_i\\in\\Gamma_m$ such that $a_i\\prec\\prec b_i$, $c_i\\prec\\prec d_i$, and $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$; also choose $r_i\\in\\Gamma_i$. For the meter label set $a_m=c_m=z_0$ and $b_m=d_m=z_1$; the two pairs $(z_0,z_1)$ and $(z_1,z_0)$ are the same two-copy meter compound up to permutation. Write $S_i^0$ for the selected entropy representative, with $S_m^0=S_m$, and put $\\Delta_i=S_i^0(b_i)-S_i^0(a_i)>0$ and $\\mu_i=S_m(d_i)-S_m(c_i)>0$. Define $S_i(x):=\\frac{\\mu_i}{\\Delta_i}\\bigl(S_i^0(x)-S_i^0(r_i)\\bigr)$. Then $S_i(r_i)=0$ and $S_i(b_i)-S_i(a_i)=\\mu_i$, matching the meter entropy increment across the chosen calibrator endpoints. For $i=m$, $\\Delta_m=\\mu_m=1$, so this agrees with the fixed meter normalization. [A1, F1, F2, choose]\n\n2.1 For a finite separated compound $A$ with factors $(t_\\ell i_\\ell,x_\\ell)$, define $S(A):=\\sum_\\ell t_\\ell S_{i_\\ell}(x_\\ell)$. The sum is finite and invariant under reordering; concatenating compounds adds the sums, and scaling every amount by $t>0$ multiplies it by $t$. The explicit-factor convention in the Statement therefore makes $S$ well-defined, additive, and extensive. [A1, step 1.1, construct]\n\n3.1 Fix a composition with amounts $M_i$ for finitely many labels and let $A,B$ be two states of that same composition. For a state $x$ of type $i$, put\n$s_i(x):=\\frac{S_i(x)-S_i(a_i)}{\\mu_i}.$\nThe same-type entropy representation and the CH for type $i$ show\n$e_x\\sim_s(1-s_i(x))e_{a_i}+s_i(x)e_{b_i}.$\nIndeed, the signed difference has total coefficient zero and $S_i$-value zero, so its positive and negative parts have equal mass and entropy and therefore compare both ways by [F2]. The calibrator relation gives\n$(e_{b_i}-e_{a_i})\\sim_s(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i})$\nby taking the signed difference of $(a_i,d_i)\\sim(b_i,c_i)$ and canceling common terms with [F3]. Add and scale these identities over the finitely many factors of $A$. With\n$q_i(A):=\\sum_{\\ell:\\,i_\\ell=i}t_\\ell s_i(x_\\ell),$\nthe resulting signed map is\n$A\\sim_s \\sum_i M_i e_{a_i}+\\sum_i q_i(A)(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i}),$\nwhere the sums range over the finite support of the composition. The same holds for $B$. Since they have the same composition, the reference base cancels, and $B-A$ is signed-equivalent to the finite meter map\n$D:=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)(e_{d_i}-e_{c_i}).$\nExtend the meter entropy linearly to finite signed maps. Then\n$S_m(D)=\\sum_i\\bigl(q_i(B)-q_i(A)\\bigr)\\mu_i=S(B)-S(A).$\nThe positive and negative parts of $D$ have equal mass. If $S(A)\\le S(B)$, the same-type entropy representation [F2] for finite meter products gives $D^-\\prec D^+$, hence $D$ compares in the forward signed direction. Translation, addition, and ordinary-order compatibility [F3] transfer this to $A\\prec B$. Reversing the entropy inequality gives the reverse comparison. Thus $S$ characterizes every same-composition comparison. Each particular comparison uses only finitely many labels; no infinite sum or uncountable product occurs. [A1, F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 The meter normalization fixes the multiplicative unit. Changing the chosen reference state for type $i$ changes only its additive constant; on a finite compound these constants contribute amount-weighted conserved-type offsets. This proves the stated freedom and completes the construction. [A1, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 2.5 and §IV.B.2, pp. 27–29, 56–59",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Decorated finite-chain costs with catalysts",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 14,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of tagged system types closed under finite composition $T\\times T'$ and positive scaling $tT$. Scaling is associative and acts componentwise on products: $s(tT)=(st)T$ and $t(T\\times T')=(tT)\\times(tT')$. Each type $T$ has a nonempty state set $X_T$ of finite tagged compounds, every state in $X_T$ is represented by the coefficient-map interpretation of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]] and has total coefficient mass $m(T)=w_T\\ge0$, and $m(T\\times T')=m(T)+m(T')$, $m(tT)=t\\,m(T)$. The entropy $\\sigma_T:X_T\\to\\mathbb R$ is the finite additive extensive entropy from [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]]; in particular $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nInclude the formal empty unit type $1$, with $X_1=\\{*\\}$, $m(1)=0$, $\\sigma_1(*)=0$, $T\\times1=T$, and $t1=1$. A transition edge from type $T$ to type $T'$ is a pair $a\\in X_T$, $b\\in X_{T'}$ such that the underlying equal-mass compounds satisfy $a\\prec b$ under [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]]. Thus an edge can exist only when $m(T)=m(T')$. Its cost is the finite real number $\\sigma_{T'}(b)-\\sigma_T(a)$.\n\nA decorated finite chain from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ consists of a catalyst type $C\\in\\mathcal G$, an integer $N\\ge1$, types $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, and states $a_k\\in X_{T_k}$, $b_{k+1}\\in X_{T_{k+1}}$ with an edge $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$ for every $0\\le k<N$. Its cost is the finite real sum\n\n$$c=\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\bigl(\\sigma_{T_{k+1}}(b_{k+1})-\\sigma_{T_k}(a_k)\\bigr).$$\n\nDefine $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ as the infimum of all such witness costs. Set $F=+\\infty$ if there is no witness and $F=-\\infty$ if the witness costs are unbounded below. The infimum is over real witness costs, so no subtraction of extended-real infima is used. Type permutations, reassociation, reversible identifications, and the identity $T\\times1=T$ are part of the declared type structure. The cost identities and inequalities require a separate proof.",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, Eqs. 6.6, 6.9, and 6.10, pp. 74–75",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Algebraic properties of decorated-chain costs",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 15,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For the cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, $F$ is positively homogeneous, invariant under adding the same catalyst to both endpoints, and satisfies the triangle inequality whenever the extended-real sum on its right is defined. It is subadditive under composition whenever $F(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)+F(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$ is defined. Every pair of actual forward and reverse decorated-chain witnesses has nonnegative total cost. A finite reverse witness therefore bounds all forward costs below; $F=-\\infty$ can occur only when the reverse direction has no witness.",
        "proof": "1.1 A reflexive identity edge from any nonempty state of $\\Gamma$ to itself has cost zero, so $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)\\le0$. For a decorated closed chain, start with the compound containing every edge input $a_0,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$. Apply the edge $a_0\\prec b_1$ with all other edge inputs as spectators, then apply $a_1\\prec b_2$ with the already produced $b_1$ and all still-unused inputs as spectators, and continue through $a_{N-1}\\prec b_N$. Consistency and transitivity give $\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}a_k\\prec\\sum_{k=1}^{N}b_k$. Since $T_N=T_0$ and the intermediate types occur once on each side, these two compounds have the same total type up to the declared reassociation and permutation. Their entropy difference is exactly the chain cost. The same-type entropy representation [F2] makes this difference nonnegative. Hence every closed-chain witness costs at least zero and $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$. [A1, F1, F2, construct]\n\n1.2 Take finite witnesses for $(\\Gamma_1,\\Gamma'_1)$ and $(\\Gamma_2,\\Gamma'_2)$. Run the first chain with a fixed state of $\\Gamma_2$ and its second catalyst as spectators; then run the second chain with the first chain's output state and first catalyst as spectators. Consistency allows both sequences, and each fixed spectator contributes zero entropy difference. The resulting witness from $\\Gamma_1\\times\\Gamma_2$ to $\\Gamma'_1\\times\\Gamma'_2$ has the sum of the two costs. If both infima are finite real numbers, choose witnesses within $\\epsilon/2$ of each infimum and let $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$. If one infimum is $-\\infty$ and the other is finite, use a fixed finite witness for the latter and arbitrarily negative witnesses for the former; the product costs are unbounded below. If both are $-\\infty$, choose both costs arbitrarily negative, so the product infimum is $-\\infty$. If either is $+\\infty$ and the other is not $-\\infty$, the right side is $+\\infty$ and the inequality is automatic. The mixed extended sum $+\\infty+(-\\infty)$ is undefined and is excluded by the Statement. [A1, F1, F2, construct]\n\n1.3 A witness for $F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)$ is a witness for $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ with enlarged catalyst $C_0\\times C$, so $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)$. Conversely, add one fixed state of the nonempty catalyst $C_0$ to every edge of an arbitrary witness for $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Its cost is unchanged, proving the reverse inequality. Thus $F(\\Gamma\\times C_0,\\Gamma'\\times C_0)=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. [A1, F1, F2]\n\n2.1 Scaling every state, type, and catalyst in a witness by $t>0$ multiplies its cost by $t$ because $\\sigma_{tT}(ta)=t\\sigma_T(a)$. This gives $F(t\\Gamma,t\\Gamma')\\le tF(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$; applying the same argument with scale $1/t$ gives equality, including the extended values. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Concatenate a witness from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ with one from $\\Gamma'$ to $\\Gamma''$, adding the other chain's fixed catalyst state as spectator to each edge. The result is a witness from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma''$ whose cost is the sum. Taking finite approximations proves the triangle inequality when both costs are finite; an unbounded-below term with a finite witness in the other direction gives the corresponding $-\\infty$ case; if a term is $+\\infty$ and the sum is defined, the inequality is automatic. For an actual forward witness and an actual reverse witness with possibly different catalysts, add the reverse catalyst's fixed state to every forward edge and the forward catalyst's fixed state to every reverse edge. Consistency supplies these padded edges, and additivity makes each edge cost unchanged. The padded chains now have the same catalyst, so concatenate them into a closed chain; step 1.1 gives $c+d\\ge0$. Thus any fixed finite reverse witness of cost $d$ bounds every forward witness below by $-d$. [A1, F1, F2, step 1.1, construct] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, Eqs. 6.6–6.16, pp. 74–75",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Catalyst-chain cost characterizes cross-type transitions",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 16,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-forward-spectator-cancellation": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the calibrated entropy family of [[thm-tdmath-arbitrary-family-calibration]], the cross-type accessibility axioms A1–A6 of [[def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses]], and the decorated cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]]. For every pair of types $\\Gamma,\\Gamma'$ and states $x\\in X_\\Gamma$, $y\\in X_{\\Gamma'}$,\n\n$$x\\prec y\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le \\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y).$$\n\nThe infimum defining $F$ need not be attained. If $F=+\\infty$, the right side is false; if $F=-\\infty$, the right side is true.",
        "proof": "1.1 If $x\\prec y$, the one-edge witness with an identity catalyst has cost $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$. Hence $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\le\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$, which is the forward inequality, including the case $F=+\\infty$ where no such transition can exist. [F1, F2]\n\n1.2 Conversely assume the displayed entropy inequality. It is impossible when $F=+\\infty$. For each integer $n\\ge1$, choose a decorated witness of real cost $c_n\\le\\Delta\\sigma+h/n$, where $\\Delta\\sigma=\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)$ and $h=\\sigma(z_1)-\\sigma(z_0)>0$ is the entropy gap of a fixed strict meter pair from [F1]. Such witnesses exist from the definition of infimum, also if $F=-\\infty$. Write their types as $T_0=\\Gamma\\times C,T_1,\\ldots,T_N=\\Gamma'\\times C$, their edges as $a_k\\prec b_{k+1}$, and their endpoints as $a_0=(\\widetilde x,c_0)$, $b_N=(\\widetilde y,d_0)$. [F1, F2, F3, choose]\n\n2.1 Put $\\delta_n=1/n$. In one finite compound type form\n$L_n=(x,b_N,b_1,\\ldots,b_{N-1},\\delta_n z_0),\\qquad R_n=(a_0,y,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1},\\delta_n z_1).$\nThese have the same composition: both contain $\\Gamma$, $\\Gamma'$, the catalyst $C$, every intermediate type $T_1,\\ldots,T_{N-1}$, and one scaled meter state, up to reassociation and permutation. Additivity of $\\sigma$ and the definition of chain cost give\n$\\sigma(R_n)-\\sigma(L_n)=\\Delta\\sigma-c_n+\\delta_n h\\ge0.$\nThus the same-composition entropy representation [F1] gives $L_n\\prec R_n$. [A1, F1, F3, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n3.1 Starting from $R_n$, use consistency on $a_0\\prec b_1$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators. The result contains $b_1$ in place of $a_0$. Next use $a_1\\prec b_2$ with $y,\\delta_nz_1,b_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_{N-1}$ as spectators, and continue in order. At stage $k$, the current compound contains $a_k$, the earlier outputs $b_1,\\ldots,b_k$, all later inputs $a_{k+1},\\ldots,a_{N-1}$, and the unchanged $y,\\delta_nz_1$. After the last edge, transitivity gives $R_n\\prec R'_n$, where $R'_n=(b_1,\\ldots,b_N,y,\\delta_nz_1)$. Compose this with $L_n\\prec R_n$ from step 2.1. The compounds $L_n$ and $R'_n$ share the finite spectator $b_1+\\cdots+b_N$; cancel it by [F4]. The result is $x+\\delta_nz_0\\prec y+\\delta_nz_1$ for every $n$. [A1, F4, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 Since $\\delta_n\\downarrow0$, stability in [A1] with bases $x,y$ and fixed positive spectators $z_0,z_1$ yields $x\\prec y$. This proves the reverse implication without assuming that any infimum is attained. [A1, step 3.1] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, Theorem 6.1 and proof, pp. 75–76",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "Cost-zero type offsets and their difference space",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 16,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra"
        ],
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For the type set $\\mathcal G$ and cost $F$ of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], define the relation $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ by $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')=F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)=0$. Let $\\mathcal P:=\\mathcal G\\times\\mathcal G$ be the set of raw type pairs. Define a relation on raw pairs by\n\n$$(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\exists C\\text{ such that }A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C.$$\n\nThe separate well-definedness lemma proves that $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with composition and positive scaling, that $\\equiv$ is an equivalence on $\\mathcal P$, and that the following operations are well-defined on its classes. After that proof, write $V_F:=\\mathcal P/\\equiv$. Define addition by $[(A,B)]+[(D,E)]:=[(A\\times D,B\\times E)]$, negation by $-[(A,B)]:=[(B,A)]$, and positive scaling by $t[(A,B)]:=[(tA,tB)]$. Set zero to $[(1,1)]$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. The same lemma establishes that these operations make $V_F$ a real vector space; no vector-space law is assumed by this Definition.",
        "proof": "not-applicable",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–79",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "The cost-zero offset construction is a real vector space",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 17,
        "deps": [
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For the construction in [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]], $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with product and positive scaling; $V_F$ is a real vector space and its displayed operations are representative-independent.",
        "proof": "1.1 The relation $\\approx_0$ is reflexive because $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$, and symmetric by definition. Suppose $\\Gamma\\approx_0\\Gamma'$ and $\\Gamma'\\approx_0\\Gamma''$. The triangle inequality gives both directed costs between $\\Gamma$ and $\\Gamma''$ at most zero. For any $\\epsilon>0$, choose a finite reverse witness of cost $d<\\epsilon$; such witnesses exist because the reverse infimum is zero. Every forward witness of cost $c$ closes with it, so [F2] gives $c+d\\ge0$, hence $c>-\\epsilon$. Letting $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ shows every forward witness has nonnegative cost and its infimum is at least zero; the same argument reverses the direction. Both infima are therefore zero, proving transitivity. The same argument proves product compatibility: subadditivity makes both product costs at most zero, and any finite reverse witness bounds every forward product witness below by a quantity tending to zero. Positive homogeneity gives scaling compatibility directly. Thus $\\approx_0$ is an equivalence compatible with product and positive scaling, and the quotient is a commutative monoid. [F1, F2, algebra]\n\n1.2 Reversible splitting [A1] gives accessible transitions in both directions between $(s+t)\\Gamma$ and $s\\Gamma\\times t\\Gamma$. Extensivity makes each such edge have zero entropy cost. Any reverse witness bounds all forward costs below by [F2]'s closed-cycle inequality, so both infima are zero. Hence $(s+t)\\Gamma\\approx_0 s\\Gamma\\times t\\Gamma$. The empty type $1$ is an identity under composition; its reassociation and permutation identifications have zero cost by the type conventions. [A1, F1, F2]\n\n2.1 The pair relation $\\equiv$ is reflexive using unit catalyst $1$ and symmetric by swapping the two sides. Suppose $(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')$ via catalyst $C$, and $(A',B')\\equiv(A'',B'')$ via catalyst $D$. Their witnesses are\n$A\\times B'\\times C\\approx_0 A'\\times B\\times C,\\qquad\nA'\\times B''\\times D\\approx_0 A''\\times B'\\times D.$\nMultiply the first equivalence by $B''\\times D$, the second by $B\\times C$, and compose. Reassociating and permuting gives\n$A\\times B''\\times(B'\\times C\\times D)\\approx_0 A''\\times B\\times(B'\\times C\\times D),$\nwhich witnesses $(A,B)\\equiv(A'',B'')$. Hence $\\equiv$ is an equivalence relation. [F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 If $(A,B)\\equiv(A',B')$ via $C$, multiplying its witness by any type $D$ shows $(A\\times D,B\\times D)\\equiv(A'\\times D,B'\\times D)$. Applying this to each summand proves addition is independent of representatives. Compatibility of $\\approx_0$ with positive scaling from step 1.1 similarly proves positive scalar multiplication is independent of representatives. Product commutativity and associativity make addition commutative and associative; swapping $(A,B)$ gives an additive inverse, and $(1,1)$ is an additive identity. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1]\n\n4.1 For $t>0$, the displayed operation $t(A,B)=(tA,tB)$ satisfies $t(v+w)=tv+tw$ because scaling distributes over type composition, and $t(sv)=(ts)v$ for $s>0$ by associativity of type scaling. Also $1v=v$. The scalar-addition law for positive $s,t$ follows from step 1.2:\n$(s+t)[A,B]=[(s+t)A,(s+t)B]=[sA\\times tA,sB\\times tB]=s[A,B]+t[A,B].$\nThe zero scalar is defined as the additive identity, and $(-t)v$ is defined as $t(-v)$, where $-v$ swaps the pair. [F1, step 1.2, step 3.1]\n\n5.1 The positive scalar laws extend to negative scalars by swapping pairs. For the scalar-addition law with opposite signs, write $s-t$ with $s,t\\ge0$. If $s\\ge t$, positive scalar addition gives $sv=(s-t)v+tv$, so cancellation in the additive group gives $(s-t)v=sv-tv$; if $t>s$, apply the same identity to $t=(t-s)+s$ and negate. Cases with zero are immediate. For scalar composition, write each nonzero scalar as its sign times a positive magnitude; the defined action swaps the pair once for each negative sign and scales by the product of magnitudes, so $(rs)v=r(sv)$, with zero cases immediate. For distributivity over vector addition, positive scalars distribute by step 4.1 and a negative scalar distributes by taking the additive inverse of that identity. Together with the abelian-group laws in step 3.1, these are all real-vector-space axioms. [F1, step 4.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–79",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, Chapters 2 and 4, complete 607-page edition",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-tdmath-splitting-cost-quotient",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "physical_scope": null,
        "title": "No-sinks cost offsets give a weak global entropy",
        "reference": "research/plan-thermodynamics-track.md#L443-L477",
        "design_state": "Draft carrier authored; scoped checks pass; prerequisite readiness is escalated; independent mathematical review pending.",
        "dependency_level": 18,
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra",
          "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space",
          "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined",
          "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference",
          "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-forward-sector-hypotheses": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-decorated-cost-algebra": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space-well-defined": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-tdmath-infinite-no-sinks-sandwich": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $F$ be the decorated cost of [[def-tdmath-decorated-chain-costs]], and assume the **no-sinks condition**\n\n$$F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)<+\\infty$$\n\nfor every type pair. Let $V_F$ be the real difference space of [[def-tdmath-cost-offset-difference-space]]. Then there is an additive extensive offset $B:\\mathcal G\\to\\mathbb R$ such that\n\n$$-F(\\Gamma',\\Gamma)\\le B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma').$$\n\nIf every admitted cross-type transition $x\\prec y$ from $\\Gamma$ to $\\Gamma'$ satisfies the calibrated cost inequality of [[thm-tdmath-unattained-cost-entropy-inference]], then $\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)$ is nondecreasing across every such transition. Only this weak inequality is asserted; no-gap equalities and strict extensions are not concluded.",
        "proof": "**Given:** [A1], [F1]–[F4], and the no-sinks condition.\n\n1.1 If $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')<+\\infty$, no-sinks gives a finite reverse witness of some real cost $c$. Concatenating it with any forward witness makes a closed chain, so [F2] gives forward cost at least $-c$. Thus $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')>-\\infty$ whenever it is finite; all cost values are in $\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}$. [A1, F2, given]\n\n2.1 For a pair class $v=[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']\\in V_F$, define $p(v):=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Suppose $(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')\\equiv(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$ via $C$, so $P=\\Gamma\\times\\Lambda'\\times C\\approx_0 Q=\\Lambda\\times\\Gamma'\\times C$. Put $D=\\Gamma'\\times\\Lambda'\\times C$. Since $P\\approx_0 Q$, the triangle inequality gives $F(P,D)\\le F(Q,D)$ and $F(Q,D)\\le F(P,D)$; the costs have no $-\\infty$ by step 1.1, so these inequalities imply equality. Catalyst invariance from [F2] gives $F(P,D)=F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$ and $F(Q,D)=F(\\Lambda,\\Lambda')$. Therefore $p$ is independent of the pair representative. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 The identity $F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma)=0$ gives $p(0)=0$. Homogeneity of $F$ gives $p(tv)=tp(v)$ for $t>0$, and its product subadditivity gives $p(v+w)\\le p(v)+p(w)$. The no-sinks condition says $p(v)<+\\infty$ exactly when $p(-v)<+\\infty$. By step 1.1, $p$ has no $-\\infty$ value. [F1, F2, given, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 Apply [F3] to $V_F$ and $p$. It gives a real-linear $L:V_F\\to\\mathbb R$ with $-p(-v)\\le L(v)\\le p(v)$ for all $v$. Put $B(\\Gamma)=L([\\Gamma,1])$, where $1$ is the empty unit type. Since $[\\Gamma\\times\\Gamma',1]=[\\Gamma,1]+[\\Gamma',1]$, $B$ is additive. Since $[t\\Gamma,1]=t[\\Gamma,1]$ (the unit type satisfies $t1=1$), $B(t\\Gamma)=tB(\\Gamma)$ for $t>0$. Also $[\\Gamma,\\Gamma']=[\\Gamma,1]-[\\Gamma',1]$, so applying the sandwich to this pair gives the displayed two-sided cost bound. [A1, F1, F3, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 For an admitted transition, [F4] gives $\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)-\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)\\ge F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$, while step 3.1 gives $B(\\Gamma)-B(\\Gamma')\\le F(\\Gamma,\\Gamma')$. Subtracting the latter from the former yields\n$[\\sigma_{\\Gamma'}(y)+B(\\Gamma')]-[\\sigma_\\Gamma(x)+B(\\Gamma)]\\ge0.$\nThus the offset entropy is nondecreasing across every admitted transition. [F4, step 3.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §VI.B, pp. 76–80",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
